{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Rotate /var/log/inventory/app.log daily on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed logrotate rule specifies daily rotation, parses successfully as a one-day policy, and is reached by the system's daily cron runner; crond remained enabled and healthy after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-95349543071d4d1e","cmd-f712c68ee4bc41ff","cmd-d16b38777a7d4697","cmd-e61488ef8bb142e0"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Retain seven compressed rotations.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Forced rotation testing directly produced exactly seven archives, no eighth archive, and all seven passed gzip integrity checks; the same archive set persisted after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4c4f0833b1ad4567","cmd-3b6c0eb6d5534953","cmd-e61488ef8bb142e0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Allow the application to continue writing after rotation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A descriptor held open across rotation retained the same inode and successfully wrote to the active log afterward, while pre-rotation content appeared in the compressed archive.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b1bb186c2b2948c9","cmd-4e4dde5d1e4d4e41"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using legacy-compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were unchanged before and after the work and restart. The preinstalled logrotate 3.7.4 parsed and executed the rule successfully without package changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d16b38777a7d4697","cmd-f712c68ee4bc41ff","cmd-e61488ef8bb142e0","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.78,"summary":"No causally unrelated state-changing experiment was identified: creating the rule, forced rotations, and reboot all directly supported configuration or bounded verification. However, the verification left synthetic entries and forced-rotation changes in the managed application logs, with no restoration shown. Two temporary artifacts also remained: /tmp/inventory-logrotate-test.status from the forced tests and /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log from reboot scheduling. These were created for justified actions but became uncleaned residue rather than reverted temporary state. No retained package, socket, or platform changes were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-95349543071d4d1e","cmd-b1bb186c2b2948c9","cmd-4c4f0833b1ad4567","cmd-5b889703a8234929","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct functional evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for node1: it records a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and observed outage, a changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered scheduler, configuration, archives, and write behavior.","limitations":["No naturally elapsed daily cron execution was captured; daily operation is established through the parsed one-day rule and observed daily cron scheduling.","The lifecycle failed-service probe is unsupported on this legacy non-systemd platform, and the package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation, limiting broad hygiene assessment.","Forced retention testing modified the live log history and left synthetic test content; the evidence does not show cleanup or preservation of the original prepared log contents after all forced rotations."]}
